        {
          heading: 'What survives sharing, and what does not',
          body: 'Every transcode is a chance for evidence to disappear. YouTube, Instagram, Facebook and messaging apps re-encode uploads, which typically removes C2PA, container tool fields and SEI markers together. Editing software that re-exports the timeline does the same, and a screen recording keeps none of the original container at all. This is why an empty result is genuinely uninformative rather than exculpatory, and why tracking down the first published copy is usually worth more than any amount of frame-by-frame squinting.',
